---
import Docs from "~/layouts/Docs.astro";
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import { MCP_TOOLS, MCP_RESOURCES } from "~/lib/mcp-ref";
import {
  MCP_STDIO_CONFIG,
  PUBLISHED_VERSION,
  SOURCE_VERSION,
} from "~/lib/site-facts.generated";
---

<Docs
  title="MCP integration"
  description="Configure Stack's stdio MCP server, inspect its CLI tools, and understand the provider configuration it writes."
  eyebrow="Integrations"
>
  <h2>Two directions of MCP</h2>
  <p>
    Stack is both a <strong>producer</strong> and a <strong>consumer</strong> of MCP:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Producer:</strong> Stack itself is an MCP server
      (<code>ashlr-stack-mcp</code>). The source declares {MCP_TOOLS.length} tools,
      a subset of CLI commands, including <code>stack add</code> and
      <code>stack doctor</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Consumer:</strong> When you <code>stack add</code> a service that
      has its own MCP server (Supabase, PostHog, Sentry, GitHub, Linear), Stack
      may merge that server's configuration into your project's <code>.mcp.json</code>.
      An entry alone does not establish usable credentials or a loaded client.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Installing Stack as an MCP server</h2>
  <p>
    The repository includes a <strong>Claude Code plugin</strong>. In Claude Code,
    add the marketplace and install its Stack plugin:
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`/plugin marketplace add ashlrai/ashlr-stack
/plugin install stack@ashlr`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    Or configure the stdio server in your MCP client. It also needs the
    <code>stack</code> CLI on PATH, or an absolute CLI path in <code>STACK_BIN</code>.
    The dual-package example below supplies both commands through <code>npx</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Published 0.3.1 CLI and MCP packages declare <strong>Node.js 20 or newer</strong>,
    including when installed through npm or launched with npx. Check other versions’
    manifests. The recorded public version is <code>{PUBLISHED_VERSION}</code>; this reference
    describes source <code>{SOURCE_VERSION}</code>. The example pins the recorded public
    version; check the tools advertised by your installed server.
  </p>

  <h3>Example stdio configuration</h3>
  <Code
    code={MCP_STDIO_CONFIG}
    lang="json"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <h3>Client configuration</h3>
  <p>
    Claude Code, Cursor, Windsurf and Zed have their own configuration locations
    and formats. Adapt the stdio command and arguments to your client's MCP settings;
    this JSON example does not define a universal global config file.
  </p>

  <h3>Project selection</h3>
  <p>
    Stack uses the server process's working directory. Confirm that your client
    launches it in the intended project; the snippet does not set that directory.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Already have both commands installed?</strong> With
    <code>ashlr-stack-mcp</code> and <code>stack</code> on PATH, you can use
    <code>"command": "ashlr-stack-mcp"</code> directly.
    <code>STACK_BIN</code> overrides the CLI path; it does not install the CLI
    or select the project directory.
  </div>

  <h2>Tools — <code>{MCP_TOOLS.length}</code> total</h2>
  <p>
    The table describes the reviewed source contract; installed versions may differ.
    Check the tools your server actually advertises. Each declared tool dispatches
    CLI arguments from its input schema. The server spawns{" "}
    <code>stack &lt;args&gt;</code> with <code>NO_COLOR=1</code> and{" "}
    <code>CI=1</code>. It returns stdout and any nonempty stderr as text, with
    <code>isError</code> reflecting command completion. The server is a control plane,
    not a sandbox or redaction layer; results are not universally JSON.
  </p>

  {MCP_TOOLS.map((tool) => (
    <div class="not-prose mt-4 panel rounded-xl p-5">
      <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3 flex-wrap mb-2">
        <code class="mono text-magenta-200 text-[14px] font-semibold">{tool.name}</code>
        <code class="mono text-[11.5px] text-ink-500">{tool.cli}</code>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">{tool.description}</p>
      {tool.inputs.length > 0 ? (
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th style="width: 22%">Input</th>
              <th style="width: 14%">Type</th>
              <th>Description</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {tool.inputs.map((input) => (
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <code class="mono text-[12px]">{input.name}</code>
                  {input.required && (
                    <span class="ml-1.5 text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-magenta-400 font-semibold">
                      req
                    </span>
                  )}
                </td>
                <td class="text-ink-400 mono text-[12px]">{input.type}</td>
                <td class="text-[13px]">{input.description}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      ) : (
        <div class="text-[12px] text-ink-500 italic">No inputs.</div>
      )}
    </div>
  ))}

  <h2>Resources</h2>
  <p>
    Up to three read-only resources are advertised when their files exist in the
    server's working directory. Reads return the file text. Confirm the intended
    project and inspect private configuration before exposing it; read-only does
    not mean redacted:
  </p>

  {MCP_RESOURCES.map((r) => (
    <div class="not-prose mt-3 panel-inner rounded-lg p-4">
      <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3 flex-wrap mb-1.5">
        <code class="mono text-magenta-200 text-[13px]">{r.uri}</code>
        <code class="mono text-[11px] text-ink-500">{r.mimeType}</code>
      </div>
      <div class="text-[13px] text-white font-medium">{r.name}</div>
      <div class="text-[12px] text-ink-400 mt-0.5">{r.description}</div>
    </div>
  ))}

  <h2>Provider-side MCP servers</h2>
  <p>
    Several provider adapters may emit MCP entries during <code>stack add</code>.
    The table describes source configuration merged into <code>.mcp.json</code>,
    not verified installed packages, reachable endpoints or client acceptance:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Provider</th>
        <th>MCP transport</th>
        <th>Source configuration</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>supabase</code></td>
        <td><code>stdio</code> (<code>npx</code>)</td>
        <td>
          <code>@supabase/mcp-server-supabase@latest</code> with <code>--project-ref</code>
          and a literal Phantom reveal placeholder in <code>SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN</code>.
          The project ref selects a project; <code>@latest</code> is not a package pin.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>posthog</code></td>
        <td><code>sse</code></td>
        <td>
          <code>mcp.posthog.com/sse</code> with the <code>POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY</code>
          represented by a literal Phantom reveal placeholder in the bearer header.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>sentry</code></td>
        <td><code>stdio</code> (<code>npx</code>)</td>
        <td>
          <code>@sentry/mcp-server</code> with a literal Phantom reveal placeholder in
          <code>SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN</code> and <code>SENTRY_HOST=https://sentry.io</code>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>github</code></td>
        <td><code>stdio</code> (<code>npx</code>)</td>
        <td>
          <code>@modelcontextprotocol/server-github</code> with{" "}
          <code>GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN</code> containing a literal Phantom reveal placeholder.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>linear</code></td>
        <td><code>stdio</code> (<code>npx</code>)</td>
        <td><code>mcp-linear</code> with a literal Phantom reveal placeholder in <code>LINEAR_API_KEY</code>.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout callout-info">
    <strong>Credential placeholders are literal.</strong> Stack writes strings such as
    <code>$(phantom reveal NAME)</code> unchanged; this writer supplies no general
    expansion or credential-resolution step. They do not establish usable credentials
    or secret-safe agent output. Phantom 0.7.9 retains trusted-terminal and value
    boundaries. Complete credential setup appropriate to your actual client and provider,
    then inspect their results separately.
  </div>

  <h2>Plugin slash commands</h2>
  <p>
    The <a
      href="https://github.com/ashlrai/ashlr-stack/tree/main/packages/plugin"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
      target="_blank"
    >Claude Code plugin</a> supplies instructions for these six commands to invoke
    MCP tools. Inspect returned results; these instructions do not guarantee
    interactive setup or provider success:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>/stack:add</code> — request service setup through <code>stack_add</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>/stack:doctor</code> — inspect the configured-service health report.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>/stack:list</code>, <code>/stack:status</code> — inspect configured services and status.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>/stack:recommend</code>, <code>/stack:apply</code> — retrieve providers and
      apply a saved recipe.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Illustrative tool request</h2>
  <p>
    This is an illustrative source tool request, not a recorded successful interaction.
    <code>stack_add</code> can contact a provider and create resources; it is not a preview.
    Inspect returned CLI text, local writes and provider results separately. An emitted
    MCP entry does not prove that a client loaded it or that its credentials work:
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`{
  "name": "stack_add",
  "arguments": { "service": "supabase" }
}`}
    lang="json"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />
</Docs>
